-- title:
--   Finiteness of the lattice points whose rescaling lies in a ball
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be the integer lattice. Fix a scale $s > 0$, a radius $R \in \mathbb{R}$ and a point $u \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Then the set of lattice indices whose rescaled positions lie in the ball of radius $sR$ about $u$,
--   $$\bigl\{ w \in \mathbb{Z}^d \ :\ \|s w - u\| < s R \bigr\},$$
--   is finite.
--
--   This is local finiteness of the lattice stated in the scale-covariant form the arguments actually need: the lattice is indexed by $\mathbb{Z}^d$ but embedded in $\mathbb{R}^d$ at mesh $s$, and the condition is expressed in terms of the index $w$ rather than of the point $sw$, so that sums indexed by lattice sites over a ball are finite at every scale.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypothesis is placed on $R$; for $R \le 0$ the set is empty and the claim is trivial. The ball is open (strict inequality), and $\|\cdot\|$ is the Euclidean norm.
-- source:
